00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:05.820
When you’re building Appian applications,
you’ll come across situations

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.370
where you need to display the same
interface component or layout—for

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:13.369
example, a card designed a certain
way—for a dynamic list of items.

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:18.439
This is where you’ll use the
looping function a!forEach[].

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.980
For each item in a list, it evaluates
an expression and gives the result.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.410
It’s especially helpful when you
don’t know how many items are in that

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.540
list, or the number changes often.

00:00:28.009 --> 00:00:31.550
So, you could use it to dynamically
display repeated interface components,

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:36.449
create user filters from record data, or
calculate values across a list of records.

00:00:37.109 --> 00:00:40.520
There are a lot of ways to use
this function, so get creative!

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.050
Now, let’s see how it works.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.959
In an expression rule,
I’ll add a!forEach[].

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.780
It has two parameters:
items and expression.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:53.610
First, items is the array that
you want to take action on.

00:00:54.119 --> 00:00:56.620
I’ll set items as 1, 2, and 3.

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:01.180
Second, expression is the
expression that you want to apply

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.010
to each of the items in the list.

00:01:03.469 --> 00:01:07.820
So, if I simply type 1 and click
TEST, the function returns 1 for

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.610
each of the three items in my list.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.400
But when you’re using a!forEach[], you’ll
most likely need to reference the values

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.080
of the list items in your expression.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.320
So you can, for example, display
record data in an interface component.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.420
You’ll do this with function variables.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.840
Function variables are available within
certain functions and have the domain fv!.

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:30.560
Use them as inputs to your expression.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.960
There are a few different function
variables in a!forEach, like

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.190
fv!itemCount and fv!isFirst, that you
can use to reference the list items.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.729
But let’s focus on fv!item,
which is the most common one.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:50.280
Fv!item lets you reference
the current item in the list.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:56.270
So, in the expression parameter,
if I replace 1 with fv!item, now

00:01:56.270 --> 00:02:01.060
the function returns 1, 2, and
3—which are the items in the list.

00:02:01.599 --> 00:02:06.339
You can also use fv!item to transform
your data in the way you need.

00:02:06.789 --> 00:02:15.500
So, if I update the expression to fv!item
+ 1, I get 2, 3, and 4 as the result.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.829
Next, let’s see how you can
use a!forEach[] to dynamically

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.000
display interface components.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:27.090
In this scenario, Acme Automobile, a
commercial vehicle fleet, wants to display

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.100
a feed of the comments about a vehicle.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.180
a!forEach[] is a perfect
way to achieve this.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:36.370
It’s often used in interfaces to
display repeated components when

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:38.019
you don’t have a set amount of data.

00:02:38.509 --> 00:02:41.159
In this case, each vehicle
can have a different number of

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.580
comments on it, and that number
varies as new comments are added.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.320
To get started, I first built a child
interface for a single comment that

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.120
I’ll call into my parent interface.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:54.929
I want to repeat this interface
for each comment on a vehicle.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:58.730
It includes the user who added
the comment, a picture of their

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:03.260
avatar, the date and time of the
comment, and the comment itself.

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:09.070
In the parent interface, I already
added my local variable, local!comments.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:13.150
Using the rule input vehicle
ID, a query gets all of the

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:14.830
comments on a particular vehicle.

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:19.720
I also set up the section layout,
so in the contents parameter, I

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:23.769
can go ahead and add a!forEach[].

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:27.970
For items, I’ll use my local
variable, local!comments.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.010
This way, the items in my list are
the comments on a particular vehicle.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:37.070
For expression, I’ll call the
comment interface I built.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:40.340
This interface has the rule
input comment, which is used

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:41.809
to get the comment record data.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:47.179
Because I want to use the data
for each comment, I’ll use fv!item

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:48.890
as the value for the rule input.

00:03:49.639 --> 00:03:52.900
Now, this interface repeats the
comment interface for the two

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:54.709
different comments on this vehicle.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:01.649
If I change the test value to a
different vehicle ID, you can see

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:05.450
that the interface now shows the five
different comments on that vehicle.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:06.190
Great!

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:10.829
Another benefit of using a!forEach[]
is when you need to make a change

00:04:10.829 --> 00:04:12.630
that applies to all components.

00:04:13.189 --> 00:04:16.829
So, if I decide to change the style
of the name field, I can update it

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:20.430
once in the child interface, and
the change is immediately applied

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.170
to all of the comments in the feed.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.719
It would take a lot longer if I
had separate text fields for each

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:29.009
name and had to manually update
all of the style parameters.

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:34.190
So, even if you do have a set amount of
interface components you’re repeating, you

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:38.620
can use a!forEach[] to save time building
by only configuring it once, like in

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:40.399
this key performance indicator pattern.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:43.360
a!forEach[] is a versatile function.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.499
When you’re building your apps,
think about how you can use it to

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:50.940
build more reusable, comprehensible,
and powerful rules and interfaces.

